# Cartesian Sampling Image Characteristics

**Part:** G – Image characteristics · **Reading time:** ~45 min · **Pass 1 overview:** [Contrast and Image Quality](../pass1_big_picture/P1.4_Contrast_and_Image_Quality.ipynb) · **Refreshers you may want:** [Fourier pairs and sampling](../refreshers/RM5_Fourier_Pairs_and_Sampling.ipynb), [2D Fourier and images](../refreshers/RM6_2D_Fourier_and_Images.ipynb)

**Why this chapter matters.** The most common type of pulse sequence samples k-space on a Cartesian grid using frequency and phase encoding. This chapter is the "engineering datasheet" of such a sequence: it collects, in one place, the formulas that connect the knobs on the scanner console (FOV, matrix, slice thickness, bandwidth, TR, NEX) to what you get (resolution, scan time, SNR) and to what the hardware must deliver (gradient amplitudes and durations). At the end you get a small Python **protocol calculator** that does all of this for you, with worked examples.

<<<md>>>
## Learning goals

1. Describe how images are formed
    * Determine the image FOV and resolution when using Cartesian sampling
    * Determine the scan time when using Cartesian sampling
1. Manipulate MRI sequence parameters to improve performance
    * Determine how Cartesian sequence parameters will change FOV and resolution
    * Determine how changes in sequence parameters will affect the scan time and SNR
    * Check whether a protocol is feasible on given gradient hardware, and estimate the minimum TE

<<<md>>>
## Symbols used in this chapter

| Symbol | Meaning (plain words) | SI unit | Typical value / remark |
|---|---|---|---|
| $\bar\gamma=\gamma/2\pi$ | gyromagnetic ratio | Hz/T | 42.58 MHz/T (¹H) |
| $\vec k=[k_x,k_y,k_z]$ | k-space position, $\vec k(t)=\bar\gamma\int_0^t\vec G(\tau)d\tau$ | m⁻¹ (cycles/m) | |
| $\Delta k_x,\Delta k_y$ | k-space sample spacing in x / y | m⁻¹ | ~4 m⁻¹ |
| $k_{x,max}, k_{y,max}$ | largest k sampled in x / y | m⁻¹ | ~500 m⁻¹ |
| $FOV_x, FOV_y$ | field of view in readout / phase-encode direction | m | 0.2–0.5 m |
| $\delta_x,\delta_y,\delta_z$ | spatial resolution (voxel size) in x, y, z | m | 0.5–5 mm |
| $\Delta z$ | slice thickness ($=\delta_z$ in 2D) | m | 1–10 mm |
| $N_{read}$ | number of readout samples (original: $N_{FE}$) | – | 128–512 |
| $N_{PE}$ | number of phase-encoding steps | – | 128–512 |
| $G_{x,r}$ | readout gradient amplitude (original figure: $G_{xr}$) | T/m | 2–30 mT/m |
| $G_{y,PE}$ | largest phase-encoding gradient amplitude (figure: $G_{yp}$) | T/m | 5–30 mT/m |
| $G_{yi}$ | phase-encoding amplitude increment between steps | T/m | ~0.1 mT/m |
| $t_y$ | duration of the phase-encoding gradient lobe | s | 0.5–2 ms |
| $G_{z,SS}$ | slice-select gradient amplitude | T/m | 5–30 mT/m |
| $T_{rf}$ | RF pulse duration | s | 1–4 ms |
| $TBW$ | time–bandwidth product of the RF pulse | – | 2–8 |
| $BW_{rf}=TBW/T_{rf}$ | RF pulse bandwidth | Hz | 1–4 kHz |
| $\Delta t$ | dwell time = ADC sampling interval (figure: $\Delta T_s$) | s | 2–20 µs |
| $BW_{read}=1/\Delta t$ | total receiver bandwidth (figure: $RBW$) | Hz | 32–500 kHz |
| $BW_{pix}=BW_{read}/N_{read}$ | bandwidth per pixel | Hz | 100–1000 Hz |
| $T_{read}=N_{read}\Delta t$ | readout duration | s | 2–10 ms |
| $TE$, $TR$ | echo time, repetition time | s | |
| $\theta$ | flip angle | rad | |
| $NEX$ | number of averages (original: $N_{avg}$) | – | 1–4 |
| $N_{TR}$ | number of TRs in the scan | – | |
| $N_{slices}$ | number of slices in a 2D multi-slice scan | – | 1–60 |
| $T_{scan}$ | total scan time | s | |
| $T_{meas}$ | total ADC sampling time | s | |
| $SNR$, $f_{seq}$ | signal-to-noise ratio; sequence factor (transverse magnetization at TE relative to $M_0$) | – | |
| $\Delta V$ | voxel volume $\delta_x\delta_y\delta_z$ | m³ | |
| $G_{max}$, $SR$ | maximum gradient amplitude; maximum slew rate | T/m, T/m/s | 40 mT/m, 150 T/m/s |
| $B_0$ | main field | T | 1.5, 3 T |
| $\Delta f_{cs}$ | fat–water chemical-shift frequency | Hz | ≈ −440 Hz at 3 T |

